EBS — Elastic Block Store

EBS volume = network-attached block storage for EC2

Acts like a virtual hard drive attached to an EC2 instance
Bound to one Availability Zone
Can normally be attached to one EC2 instance at a time
EBS Multi-Attach allows certain volumes to be attached to multiple EC2 instances simultaneously
Has provisioned capacity → you choose the volume size
Persistent storage → data remains when EC2 is stopped
Can be used as an EC2 boot/root volume
Delete on termination

Controls what happens to an EBS volume when an EC2 instance is terminated:

Root volume → delete by default
Additional EBS volumes → usually preserved by default
Can be changed through the AWS Console / CLI
EBS Snapshots

Snapshot = backup of an EBS volume

Stored in Amazon S3
Can be used to create new EBS volumes
Can copy snapshots to another AZ/Region
Useful for backup, disaster recovery and migration
Snapshot features

EBS Snapshot Archive

Lower-cost storage for rarely accessed snapshots
Restoring takes significantly longer
Use when snapshots don't need to be restored frequently

Recycle Bin

Retains deleted EBS snapshots according to rules
Protects against accidental deletion

Fast Snapshot Restore (FSR)

Makes a snapshot immediately usable without the normal initialization delay
Costs extra
Useful when you need fast access after creating a volume from a snapshot
AMI — Amazon Machine Image

AMI = template used to launch EC2 instances

Contains things such as:

OS
Applications/software
Configuration
Monitoring tools
Other required settings

Benefits:

Faster and consistent EC2 deployments
Can create your own customized EC2 template
AMIs can be copied between Regions
Types of AMI
Public AMI → provided for public use
Custom AMI → created by you/your organization
AWS Marketplace AMI → provided by third-party vendors
Basic process

Create/configure EC2 → create AMI → launch new EC2 instances from AMI

EC2 Instance Store

Instance Store = temporary local storage physically attached to the EC2 host

Very high-performance storage
Lower latency than network-attached storage
Ephemeral → data can be lost when the instance is stopped/terminated or the underlying hardware fails
Good for:
Cache
Temporary data
Scratch space
Buffers

Don't use it for data that must persist.

EBS Volume Types
1. General Purpose SSD

gp3 / gp2

General-purpose workloads
Good balance of price and performance
gp3 → IOPS and throughput can be configured independently
gp2 → performance is tied more closely to volume size

Default choice for many EC2 workloads.

2. Provisioned IOPS SSD

io1 / io2

High-performance workloads
Designed especially for databases and I/O-intensive applications
IOPS can be provisioned independently from storage capacity
io2 → higher durability and performance than io1

Think:

Need consistently high IOPS → io1/io2

3. Throughput Optimized HDD

st1

Low-cost HDD
Designed for frequently accessed, throughput-intensive workloads
Examples:
Big data
Data warehouses
Log processing

Think:

Need high sequential throughput → st1

4. Cold HDD

sc1

Lowest-cost EBS option
Designed for infrequently accessed data
Lower performance than st1

Think:

Infrequently accessed + lowest cost → sc1

The EBS decision tree you actually need

This is much more important than memorizing all the numbers:

Normal EC2 workload
→ gp3

Need very high/consistent IOPS, e.g. database
→ io2

Need high sequential throughput
→ st1

Infrequently accessed data + lowest cost
→ sc1

Temporary, extremely fast local storage
→ Instance Store

Backup of EBS
→ EBS Snapshot

Need to recreate an entire configured EC2 environment
→ AMI
